---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    question: 'What is a good internet speed in 2026?',
    answer:
      'For many homes, about 100 Mbps download and 20 Mbps upload is a useful starting point. That matches the FCC fixed broadband benchmark, but it is not a rule that every household needs the same plan.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is 100 Mbps fast enough for a family?',
    answer:
      'It can be. A 100 Mbps plan gives several people room for video, browsing, and calls, but the best fit depends on how many high-resolution streams and large downloads overlap.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much upload speed do I need?',
    answer:
      'About 20 Mbps upload is a practical baseline for many households. Video calls, live streaming, cloud backups, and sending large files all use upload capacity, so heavy creators may want more.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a good ping for calls and games?',
    answer:
      'Zoom says latency of 150 ms or less and jitter of 40 ms or less are typical recommendations for calls. Games do not share one universal ping threshold, and a lower, steady ping usually feels more responsive.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why is my speed test lower than my internet plan?',
    answer:
      'A speed test measures what reached that device at that moment. Wi-Fi distance, the device, busy hours, other people using the connection, and the overhead of moving data can all reduce the measured result.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is 4K streaming 15 Mbps or 20 Mbps?',
    answer:
      'Both figures are right for different services. Netflix recommends at least 15 Mbps for UHD, while YouTube lists a sustained 20 Mbps for 4K playback, so plan on about 15 to 20 Mbps per stream.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: questions.map(({ question, answer }) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: answer },
  })),
};
---

<Base
  title="What Is a Good Internet Speed in 2026? A Plain-English Guide"
  description="A practical 2026 guide to good download, upload, and ping speeds, with sourced recommendations for streaming, calls, games, and busy households."
  breadcrumb="Good internet speed"
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <main class="wrap content-page">
    <span class="round-banner">Speed school</span>
    <h1>What Is a Good Internet Speed in 2026?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      For many homes, about 100 Mbps download and 20 Mbps upload is a useful starting point. That
      matches the <a href="https://www.fcc.gov/document/fcc-increases-broadband-speed-benchmark">FCC's 2024 fixed broadband benchmark</a>,
      but it is not a one-size-fits-all plan: lighter households can use less, while busy homes,
      creators, and people moving large files may benefit from more.
    </p>
    <article class="content-card stk">
      <h2>How fast is good for your household?</h2>
      <p>
        Begin with the people and activities that overlap, not the largest number on a plan
        advertisement. The ranges below are planning starting points, not provider guarantees or
        a promise that every device will reach the full speed over Wi-Fi. Published service
        recommendations differ: Netflix recommends at least 15 Mbps for UHD, YouTube lists 20 Mbps
        for sustained 4K playback, and Zoom publishes separate rates by call quality.
      </p>
      <div class="guide-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Starting internet speed ranges by household" tabindex="0">
        <table class="guide-table">
          <caption>Household starting ranges for a steady connection</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Household</th>
              <th scope="col">Download</th>
              <th scope="col">Upload</th>
              <th scope="col">Ping</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">1 to 2 people, light use</th>
              <td>About 25 to 50 Mbps</td>
              <td>About 5 to 10 Mbps</td>
              <td>For calls, Zoom's typical target is 150 ms or less</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">3 to 4 people, streaming</th>
              <td>About 50 to 100 Mbps</td>
              <td>About 10 to 20 Mbps</td>
              <td>Lower and steadier is better for interactive use</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">5 or more people, gamers, or creators</th>
              <td>About 100 to 300 Mbps</td>
              <td>About 20 to 50 Mbps</td>
              <td>Zoom recommends 150 ms or less; games vary</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Work from home with heavy uploads</th>
              <td>About 100 to 300 Mbps</td>
              <td>About 50 to 100 Mbps</td>
              <td>Zoom recommends 150 ms or less and steady jitter</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="guide-table-note">
        The FCC's benchmark is a national fixed-broadband benchmark, not a recommended plan tier
        for every family. The household ranges are practical estimates based on overlapping
        activities; measure your own connection and adjust for your routine. See the{' '}
        <a href="https://www.fcc.gov/document/fcc-increases-broadband-speed-benchmark">FCC's 2024 announcement</a>,{' '}
        <a href="https://help.netflix.com/en/node/306">Netflix's speed recommendations</a>, and{' '}
        <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/78358">YouTube's sustained playback rates</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The heavier-upload range is a planning estimate too. YouTube's H.264 guidance lists about
        30 Mbps for 4K at 30 frames per second and 35 Mbps at 60 frames per second, and its
        streaming tips recommend 20% spare headroom. One such stream can use roughly 36 to
        42 Mbps including that cushion; two can use about 72 to 84 Mbps before calls or other
        uploads. These are encoder settings for live video, not a household-wide requirement.
        Check <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/2853702">YouTube's live encoder settings</a>{' '}
        and <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/2853856">its network streaming tips</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What is a good upload speed?</h2>
      <p>
        A useful upload speed depends on what leaves your home connection. Zoom's published
        bandwidth requirements for a group video call range from about 1.8 Mbps up at 720p to
        about 3.0 Mbps up at 1080p. A single call is only one part of a household: backups, shared
        screens, and another person sending files can use upload at the same time.{' '}
        <a href="https://support.zoom.com/hc/en/article?id=zm_kb&amp;sysparm_article=KB0060748">Zoom lists the rates by meeting quality</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you stream live video, size upload for the stream itself and keep some spare capacity.
        YouTube's H.264 recommendations are about 30 Mbps for 4K at 30 frames per second and
        35 Mbps at 60 frames per second. Its streaming tips recommend leaving about 20 percent
        headroom. Those are encoder bitrates for those live-video settings, not a universal
        upload requirement for a video call or a home plan. Read{' '}
        <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/2853702">YouTube's live encoder settings</a>{' '}
        and <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/2853856">network streaming tips</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What is a good ping?</h2>
      <p>
        Ping is the round-trip delay for a small request, measured in milliseconds. A connection
        can have excellent download speed and still feel slow if each response takes a long time
        to arrive. Zoom describes 150 ms or less latency and 40 ms or less jitter as typical
        recommendations for calls. For games, lower and steadier ping is usually preferable, but
        the game server, route, and game itself matter, so there is no single cutoff that promises
        a good match. See{' '}
        <a href="https://support.zoom.com/hc/en/article?id=zm_kb&amp;sysparm_article=KB0070504">Zoom's guidance on latency and jitter</a>{' '}
        and <a href="https://www.playstation.com/en-us/support/games/ps4-remote-play-connection-troubleshooting/">PlayStation's Remote Play troubleshooting</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a concrete game-streaming example, PlayStation says Remote Play needs at least
        5 Mbps in each direction and recommends 15 Mbps each way for the best experience. Those
        figures apply to Remote Play, not every online multiplayer game.
      </p>

      <h2>Why does my speed test show less than my plan?</h2>
      <p>
        Your plan describes a service tier. A test shows what one device received at one moment,
        over one path through your home and your provider. A gap is not automatically a sign that
        the plan is broken. Check these common causes before changing anything.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Wi-Fi band and distance.</strong> The 2.4 GHz band can reach farther but is often
          busier; 5 GHz and 6 GHz can be faster nearby and weaken sooner through walls.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The test device.</strong> An older Wi-Fi radio, power-saving mode, background
          apps, or a browser with many open tabs can limit what the test reports.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Busy hours.</strong> Your provider's route or a shared neighborhood segment may
          be busier at one time than another.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Other people and devices.</strong> Streaming, video calls, backups, and updates
          share the same connection while a test is running.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Protocol overhead.</strong> Real network traffic includes headers, acknowledgements,
          and retransmissions. The full line rate is not all application data.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For a useful comparison, run more than one test near your router, pause other heavy
        transfers, and try a wired connection if available. Keep the device and test service the
        same when you compare results over time.
      </p>

      <h2>How can I check my own speed?</h2>
      <p>
        Run the real browser-based test in{' '}
        <a href="/">Guess the Speed</a>, or choose Dev in the Game / Dev switch at the top of the
        home screen. Add <code>?dev=1</code> to the home-page URL to open{' '}
        <a href="/?dev=1">Dev mode</a> directly with the raw measurement details. Test download
        and upload separately, note the ping, and compare the result with the room and device you
        actually use. A speed test is a snapshot, not a permanent grade for your internet.
      </p>
      <p>
        Want to add up streams, calls, and devices? Use the{' '}
        <a href="/how-much-internet-speed-do-i-need/">household speed calculator</a>.
        If the speed you see is in MB/s, the{' '}
        <a href="/mbps-to-mbs/">Mbps to MB/s converter</a> helps compare it with your plan.
      </p>

      <h2>Can guessing make a speed test more fun?</h2>
      <p>
        It can. Before you start the test, ask everyone to guess both download and upload speed.
        Then the needle reveals the answer and the closest guess wins the round. That little
        prediction turns a number on a screen into a shared moment, and it gives kids a reason to
        think about Wi-Fi, distance, and all the devices using the connection.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        {questions.map(({ question, answer }) => (
          <section>
            <h3>{question}</h3>
            <p>{answer}</p>
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="story-cta">
        <a class="go" href="/">Start a game</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
